Reusable Stata Skills for Generating Main Tables and Figures: Card & Krueger (1994) Replication
1. Overview and Purpose of Reusable Stata Workflows
To replicate the empirical framework of Card and Krueger (1994) efficiently and reproducibly, the provided Stata script (code.do) encapsulates modular, reusable routines. These routines clean raw fast-food establishment survey data (public.csv), construct core econometric indicators, generate visual distributions, and export structured regression and summary tables.

This documentation details the reusable Stata skills and modular code blocks embedded in the workflow for generating Figure 1, Table 2, Table 3, Table 4, and Table 7.


2. Reusable Skill Modules
Module A: Data Initialization and Core Variable Construction
Skill Description: Standardizes raw data input, converts text/variable names to lowercase, generates chain indicator dummies (ch_), constructs Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) labor measures across both survey waves with permanent closure adjustments (status2 == 3), computes the initial wage gap exposure variable (gap), and defines full meal price changes (dlnmeal).
Core Stata Code Block:

clear all

set more off

import delimited "public.csv", clear

rename *, lower

* Chain dummies

tab chain, gen(ch_)

* FTE definitions

gen fte1 = empft + 0.5 * emppt + nmgrs

gen fte2 = empft2 + 0.5 * emppt2 + nmgrs2

replace fte2 = 0 if status2 == 3

gen dfte = fte2 - fte1

* Wage gap construction

gen gap = 0

replace gap = (5.05 - wage_st) / wage_st if state == 1 & wage_st < 5.05 & !missing(wage_st)

replace gap = 0 if state == 1 & wage_st >= 5.05 & !missing(wage_st)

* Meal price log changes

gen meal1 = psoda + pfry + pentree

gen meal2 = psoda2 + pfry2 + pentree2

gen dlnmeal = ln(meal2) - ln(meal1)


Module B: Visualizing Starting Wage Distributions (Figure 1 Skill)
Skill Description: Generates discrete histograms with a fixed bin width ($0.05$) to illustrate starting wage distributions across states (NJ vs. PA) and survey waves (Wave 1 vs. Wave 2), capturing the sharp spike at the new $$5.05$ statutory minimum wage in New Jersey.
Core Stata Code Block:

preserve

keep if !missing(wage_st) & !missing(wage_st2)

* Figure 1A: New Jersey - Wave 1

twoway histogram wage_st if state==1, discrete width(0.05) color(black) ///

       title("Figure 1A: New Jersey Starting Wages (Feb 1992)") ///

       xtitle("Starting Wage ($)") ytitle("Percent of Stores") ///

       xlabel(4.25(0.1)5.6)

graph export "Figure1_NJ_Feb1992.png", replace

* Figure 1C: New Jersey - Wave 2

twoway histogram wage_st2 if state==1, discrete width(0.05) color(black) ///

       title("Figure 1C: New Jersey Starting Wages (Nov 1992)") ///

       subtitle("Post Minimum Wage Increase to $5.05") ///

       xtitle("Starting Wage ($)") ytitle("Percent of Stores") ///

       xlabel(4.25(0.1)5.6)

graph export "Figure1_NJ_Nov1992.png", replace

restore


Module C: Tabulating Chain Distribution & Summary Stats (Table 2 Skill)
Skill Description: Extracts categorical chain proportions using native Stata matrix operations (matcell, matrow) combined with putexcel, and computes summary statistics (means and standard deviations) via estpost summarize and esttab.
Core Stata Code Block:

* Matrix-based store type tabulation

tabulate chain if state == 1, matcell(freq_nj) matrow(cats)

tabulate chain if state == 0, matcell(freq_pa)

matrix pct_nj = freq_nj / 331 * 100

matrix pct_pa = freq_pa / 79 * 100

matrix table2 = freq_nj, pct_nj, freq_pa, pct_pa

matrix colnames table2 = NJ_Freq NJ_Pct PA_Freq PA_Pct

matrix rownames table2 = BurgerKing KFC RoyRogers Wendyes

* Summary statistics export for Table 2

eststo t2_nj: estpost summarize fte1 fte2 wage_st wage_st2 meal1 meal2 hrsopen hrsopen2 if state == 1, listwise

eststo t2_pa: estpost summarize fte1 fte2 wage_st wage_st2 meal1 meal2 hrsopen hrsopen2 if state == 0, listwise

esttab t2_nj t2_pa using "Table2_Rep.csv", replace cells("mean(fmt(2)) sd(fmt(2))") plain


Module D: Balanced Panel Filtering and Difference-in-Differences (Table 3 Skill)
Skill Description: Filters the balanced panel subset (balanced == 1), classifies New Jersey stores into initial wage tiers (nj_wage_grp), executes cross-state and intra-state summary statistics, runs two-sample $t$-tests for Difference-in-Differences (DID), and exports detailed CSV summaries.
Core Stata Code Block:

gen balanced = (!missing(fte1) & !missing(fte2))

gen nj_wage_grp = .

replace nj_wage_grp = 1 if state == 1 & wage_st == 4.25

replace nj_wage_grp = 2 if state == 1 & wage_st > 4.25 & wage_st < 5.00

replace nj_wage_grp = 3 if state == 1 & wage_st >= 5.00 & !missing(wage_st)

* Cross-state DID t-test

ttest dfte if balanced == 1, by(state)

* Export Table 3 structured stats

eststo clear

eststo t3_nj:   estpost summarize fte1 fte2 dfte if state == 1 & balanced == 1

eststo t3_pa:   estpost summarize fte1 fte2 dfte if state == 0 & balanced == 1

eststo nj_low:  estpost summarize dfte if nj_wage_grp == 1 & balanced == 1

eststo nj_mid:  estpost summarize dfte if nj_wage_grp == 2 & balanced == 1

eststo nj_high: estpost summarize dfte if nj_wage_grp == 3 & balanced == 1

esttab t3_nj t3_pa nj_low nj_mid nj_high using "Table3_Replication_Output.csv", replace ///

    cells("mean(fmt(2)) sd(fmt(2)) count(fmt(0))") plain


Module E: Reduced-Form Regression Modeling (Table 4 & Table 7 Skill)
Skill Description: Estimates robust OLS regression models for employment changes (dfte) and full meal price log changes (dlnmeal) across multiple specifications (treatment dummies, continuous wage gaps, chain/ownership controls, and regional fixed effects), alongside heterogeneity sub-sample extensions.
Core Stata Code Block:

* Table 4: Employment Change Regressions

reg dfte state if !missing(dfte) & !missing(wage_st), robust

estimates store m1

reg dfte state ch_1 ch_3 ch_4 co_owned if !missing(dfte) & !missing(wage_st), robust

estimates store m2

reg dfte gap ch_1 ch_3 ch_4 co_owned southj northj pa1 if !missing(dfte) & !missing(wage_st), robust

estimates store m5

esttab m1 m2 m5 using "regression_result.rtf", replace


3. Summary
The modular structure of code.do acts as an automated, reusable skill library in Stata. It ensures that data cleaning, variable transformations, distributional plotting, and table export can be executed seamlessly in a single pipeline, facilitating absolute reproducibility for econometric research.
